import{_ as t,o as a,c as r,a9 as o}from"./chunks/framework.asyjyako.js";const h=JSON.parse('{"title":"PkgEval","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"tools/pkgeval.md","filePath":"tools/pkgeval.md","lastUpdated":null}'),s={name:"tools/pkgeval.md"};function i(n,e,l,d,c,g){return a(),r("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[o('<h1 id="PkgEval" tabindex="-1">PkgEval <a class="header-anchor" href="#PkgEval" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;PkgEval {#PkgEval}&quot;">​</a></h1><p>Check installation and package tests of an exact registered release on Julia stable and nightly. PkgEval is an <strong>external Linux sandbox tool</strong>, used by the optional <strong>Registered PkgEval</strong> workflow; it is not a PerfChecker performance collector.</p><p>To evaluate an installed release, open <strong>Actions → Registered PkgEval → Run workflow</strong> and select <code>source_kind=registered</code>, its <code>package_version</code>, a <code>general_revision</code> and optionally the expected registered tree. The selector resolves immutable source identity before evaluation. Keep stable and nightly verdicts separate.</p><p>The <a href="https://github.com/Mirage-Interactive-Fr/PerfChecker.jl/actions/runs/38150116805" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">completed Core 1.1.0 run</a> tested registered tree <code>753e41cad1c984640a2337b314b70ad6932626ad</code> (release commit <code>4e81603c57b47bc08372992492fdf2cbbd718b3b</code>). Its <a href="https://github.com/Mirage-Interactive-Fr/PerfChecker.jl/issues/29#issuecomment-6111222715" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">recorded result</a> is:</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:right;">Runtime</th><th style="text-align:right;">Upstream status</th><th style="text-align:right;">Package assertions</th><th style="text-align:right;">Configuration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="text-align:right;">Julia 1.13.1 stable</td><td style="text-align:right;"><code>test</code></td><td style="text-align:right;">2,380 PASS; 1 Broken</td><td style="text-align:right;">Full suite; shared cache disabled; upstream compilation policy</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;">Julia 1.14.0-DEV.3606 nightly</td><td style="text-align:right;"><code>test</code></td><td style="text-align:right;">2,380 PASS; 1 Broken</td><td style="text-align:right;">Same policy, evaluated separately</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Both used a <strong>configured 90-minute sandbox budget</strong>. That is a limit, not measured duration. These results establish the selected release&#39;s package-test outcome on those runtimes; they do not measure speedups or qualify all interfaces/hardware counters.</p><details class="details custom-block"><summary>Read artifacts and choose another source</summary><p>Download <code>pkgeval-request</code>, <code>pkgeval-stable</code> and <code>pkgeval-nightly</code>. Match request source kind, version/tree, General/controller revisions and workflow identity. Inspect each <code>result.toml</code>, <code>evaluation.log</code> and <code>PkgEval-Manifest.toml</code>, including status/reason and phase-completion fields. A successful package-test status is <code>test</code>. Test-phase <code>duration_seconds</code> is CPU time in the pinned controller; the final “PkgEval … after …s” log line is total elapsed sandbox wall time. Missing/zero unavailable timing fields must not become zero-cost claims.</p><p>The completed run&#39;s stable artifact <code>11707536498</code> has SHA-256 <code>2e05e950999151f895bc11a3b00ec3a251a609ceefb76b7b50f28ad7717cf04b</code>; nightly artifact <code>11707694207</code> has SHA-256 <code>ca6fd33ed193f61330379540b6eb7046b9a024442b4e995c23bf41530e7da1a7</code>. The original receipts remain unchanged.</p><p>For an unpublished candidate use <code>source_kind=git_candidate</code>, a lowercase full 40-character <code>candidate_revision</code>, and a General revision for dependencies; leave version/expected-tree fields empty. Candidate records identify repository/revision and do not claim registered-tree verification. A candidate pass cannot repair an immutable registered archive.</p><p>Manual defaults are <code>package_images=upstream</code> and <code>time_limit_minutes=45</code>; selecting 90 gives 5,400 seconds for the complete sandbox evaluation and a separate 120-minute job limit. Setup, installation, precompilation and testing share the sandbox budget. <code>package_images=yes</code> changes compilation policy; do not equate that outcome with upstream-default qualification. Scheduled source selection/configuration is recorded in the workflow request.</p><p><a href="https://github.com/Mirage-Interactive-Fr/PerfChecker.jl/actions/runs/38145884338" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Earlier 45-minute attempts</a> remain failures: stable reached <code>kill/time_limit</code> without an observed failed assertion, while nightly failed downloading Julia (code 28) before tests. The older <a href="https://github.com/Mirage-Interactive-Fr/PerfChecker.jl/actions/runs/38044386943" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">1.0.1 evaluation</a> remains its own failed archive result. A later pass does not rewrite any of these receipts. Observer <code>cleanup_complete</code> describes observer-owned resources, not a guarantee that every process on the host stopped.</p><p>Local use requires PkgEval&#39;s Linux/kernel/container prerequisites. See its <a href="https://github.com/JuliaCI/PkgEval.jl#quick-start" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">setup instructions</a>.</p></details>',7)])])}const p=t(s,[["render",i]]);export{h as __pageData,p as default};
